### CUDA build output ###
cuda/*.exe
cuda/*.exp
cuda/*.lib
cuda/*.bin
*.exe
*.exp
cuda/*.pdb
cuda/*.obj
vc140.pdb
contrib/

# ...except the kernels that ship inside the jar, which are tracked on purpose so a
# clone builds a jar that needs nothing else installed. Without these negations the
# *.exe rule above drops them silently: the jar still builds, and every user of it
# quietly gets the 4.5x slower CPU path.
#
# This had already happened to three of the four. Only find_targets was ever negated, so
# noise_column and two_chunk_lift were dropped from every clone from the day they were
# written -- the lift being the one 6bc measured at 4.3x over 24 CPU threads and 6bl found
# to be the whole cost of a crossfind run. Nothing fails when they are missing, which is
# why it went unnoticed. Any new kernel needs a line here.
!src/main/resources/cuda/find_targets.exe
!src/main/resources/cuda/noise_column.exe
!src/main/resources/cuda/two_chunk_lift.exe
!src/main/resources/cuda/stack_enum.exe

__pycache__/
*.pyc
h17.table
h17.finds
*.table
*.finds
